plot-go reads and renders the project file format of Grace (xmgrace), a
separate program distributed under the GNU GPL, version 2 or later. It is
an independent implementation written from the file format and Grace's
documentation; it contains no Grace source code. Grace's own gracebat is
used only as an external reference in tests.

The standard PDF fonts plot-go refers to (Times-Roman, Helvetica, Symbol)
are named, not embedded; the viewer supplies them.

internal/afm/adobe-core14/ holds Adobe's Core 14 AFM font metric files,
unmodified, with Adobe's MustRead.html, which permits their use, copying
and distribution for any purpose provided that file accompanies them:
Copyright (c) 1985-1997 Adobe Systems Incorporated. All Rights Reserved.
These metrics are embedded in the plot-go program; so is MustRead.html,
which `plot-go -notices` prints together with this NOTICE and the LICENSE.

To draw text as shapes (plot-go -outline-text, and the OneFit GUI's
preview), plot-go embeds the Liberation fonts - metric-compatible with
Helvetica, Times and Courier - from github.com/go-fonts/liberation:
Digitized data copyright (c) 2010 Google Corporation, with Reserved Font
Arimo, Tinos and Cousine; Copyright (c) 2012 Red Hat, Inc., with Reserved
Font Name Liberation. They are licensed under the SIL Open Font License
1.1 (internal/glyph/OFL.txt); the go-fonts package under a BSD license
(internal/glyph/go-fonts-LICENSE). plot-go -notices prints both. The fonts
are used unmodified and are not sold on their own.

Where the URW base-35 fonts are installed as OpenType (Debian's
fonts-urw-base35, a Ghostscript dependency), plot-go reads their outlines
from the system at run time instead, to draw the very glyphs Ghostscript
puts in OneFit's PDFs. Those fonts (AGPL-3 with a font exception) are not
part of plot-go and are not distributed with it.
